@startuml seq_atari_fujibus_paths
' Hand-authored: it spans systems outside this repository (Atari client,
' Altirra emulator), so it cannot be generated. The fujinet-nio class names are
' checked against the sources by scripts/check_uml_names.py. For the generated
' structure see the "Atari SIO/NetSIO ingress" and "Work sources" diagrams.
title Atari: one SIO ingress, two carriers (and how other hosts differ)

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center

actor "Atari app" as AtariApp
participant "fujinet-nio-lib\nAtari client" as AtariLib

box "POSIX emulator carrier" #EEF7FF
participant "AltirraSDL\n+ netsio.atdevice / netsiohub" as Altirra
participant "UdpChannel\n(NetSIO wire only)" as UdpChannel
participant "AtariNetSioIngress" as NetSioIngress
end box

box "ESP32 hardware carrier" #FFF8E8
participant "SIO connector pins" as SioPins
participant "esp32::AtariSioIngress\n(owns the UartChannel)" as SioIngress
end box

box "Shared SIO ingress (portable)" #F4FFF1
participant "AtariSioIngressRouter" as Router
participant "AtariSioNioFrontend\ndevice 0x7F: Q / W / P / R" as NioFront
participant "AtariSioDiskFrontend\ndevices 0x31-0x38 (D1:-D8:)" as DiskFront
end box

box "fujinet-nio core" #F8F0FF
participant "RoutingManager\n(IRequestHandler)" as Routing
participant "VirtualDevices\nFujiDevice, FileDevice, ..." as Devices
participant "DiskService" as Disks
end box

== Both carriers feed the same router, byte by byte ==

AtariApp -> AtariLib : FujiNet API call
AtariLib -> Altirra : SIO command frame (emulator)
Altirra -> UdpChannel : NetSIO packets
UdpChannel -> NetSioIngress : datagrams
NetSioIngress -> Router : consume(byte)
AtariLib -> SioPins : SIO command frame (hardware)
SioPins -> SioIngress : UART bytes
SioIngress -> Router : consume(byte)

note over Router
FujiBus packets are raw here: no SLIP, no Channel stream.
The router decides which frontend a command belongs to.
end note

== Native NIO call (device 0x7F): a multi-step transaction ==

Router -> NioFront : W + data frame\n(raw FujiBus request, SIO checksum, at most 512 bytes)
NioFront -> Routing : handleRequest(IORequest)
Routing -> Devices : route by device ID + command
Devices --> Routing : IOResponse
Routing --> NioFront : response (encoded as a raw FujiBus packet)
AtariLib -> Router : P (poll)
Router -> NioFront : P
NioFront --> AtariLib : state (response ready)
AtariLib -> Router : R (read)
Router -> NioFront : R
NioFront --> AtariLib : response bytes + SIO checksum

== Standard disk (D1:-D8:) ==

AtariLib -> Router : read / write sector (device 0x31-0x38)
Router -> DiskFront : command
DiskFront -> Disks : sector I/O
Disks --> DiskFront : data / status
DiskFront --> AtariLib : SIO data + checksum

== Other FujiBus hosts (BBC, MS-DOS, Amiga, Python) ==

participant "Host\n(byte-pipe Channel)" as Other
participant "FujiBusTransport\n+ SlipFramer" as Transport
participant "IOService" as IOSvc

Other -> Transport : SLIP + FujiBus bytes (RS-232 / TCP / PTY / USB CDC)
Transport -> IOSvc : IORequest
IOSvc -> Routing : handleRequest
Routing -> Devices : route
Devices --> Routing : IOResponse
Routing --> IOSvc
IOSvc --> Transport : send(IOResponse)
Transport --> Other : SLIP + FujiBus response bytes

note over Other, Transport
Same FujiBus message format everywhere. Atari differs in the
transport framing: no SLIP and no FujiBusTransport; the SIO
ingress calls the RoutingManager directly.
end note

@enduml
